//@version=6
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//                              R S I   T R A D E   E N T R I E S
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//  Indicator Name : RSI Trade Entries
//  Author         : Forex_Market_Insights
//  Version        : v6.0 — July 2026
//  Pine Version   : Pine Script v6
//  Script Type    : Oscillator pane (overlay = false) with linked labels forced onto the price chart
//
//  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
//  │  A U T H O R   V E R I F I C A T I O N   D E C L A R A T I O N                          │
//  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
//  I am Forex_Market_Insights, the publisher and sole original author of this exact
//  implementation of "RSI Trade Entries." I designed the structure of this tool, wrote every
//  line of the Pine Script v6 code contained in this file, tested it across multiple asset
//  classes and timeframes, and I take full and sole ownership and responsibility for it.
//
//  Specifically, I independently designed and coded: the four-level signal architecture and the
//  distinct roles I assigned to each level (deep overbought, early resistance, early support,
//  deep oversold); the touch and cross detection routine; the per-level arming and hysteresis
//  state machine that prevents duplicate labels while the oscillator hovers on a level; the
//  dynamic bullish and bearish segment colouring of the RSI line together with its optional
//  slope-smoothing variant; the dual-pane labelling system that mirrors every signal onto the
//  candlestick chart using forced overlay placement; the label registry with automatic retirement
//  of the oldest drawings; and the complete alert framework. The visual language of the tool —
//  the wording of the four signal labels, their colour scheme, their placement above or below
//  price, and the on-pane level naming — was designed by me.
//
//  This declaration is made for this specific version of the script (v6.0, July 2026). Any
//  future modified version I publish will carry its own updated declaration in this same header.
//
//  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
//  │  O R I G I N A L   S C R I P T   I M P L E M E N T A T I O N                            │
//  │  V E R I F I C A T I O N   &   D E C L A R A T I O N                                    │
//  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
//  I verify and declare that this script is an original implementation authored from scratch by
//  Forex_Market_Insights. No portion of this code was copied, ported, decompiled, translated,
//  reverse-engineered or adapted from any other author's closed-source, invite-only, protected
//  or open-source script. No third-party library and no republished open-source script forms any
//  part of this work, and no other author's visual or labelling system was reproduced.
//
//  I acknowledge openly and specifically that the Relative Strength Index formula itself is not
//  mine. RSI is J. Welles Wilder Jr.'s publicly documented and universally taught momentum
//  oscillator, published in 1978, and it is available in Pine Script as the standard built-in
//  ta.rsi(). That public formula is the only pre-existing element used here, and it is used as a
//  raw numerical input in exactly the way any developer calls a standard library function.
//
//  Everything built on top of that number is my own: the level architecture, the signal
//  conditions, the anti-repeat state machine, the dual-pane label system, the dynamic line
//  colouring, the drawing lifecycle management and the alert design. Conceptual overlap with
//  other tools that also plot RSI is therefore unavoidable and expected, but there is no
//  source-code overlap of any kind with any other author's work.
//
//  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
//  │  W H A T   T H I S   I N D I C A T O R   D O E S                                        │
//  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
//  RSI Trade Entries turns the Relative Strength Index into a direct, readable entry-signal tool
//  by dividing the oscillator into four decision levels instead of the usual two.
//
//  DEEP OVERBOUGHT (default 80)  → "CONFIRM SELL"  — momentum is stretched to an extreme
//  RESISTANCE      (default 68)  → "SELL"          — the first meaningful overbought reaction
//  SUPPORT         (default 35)  → "BUY"           — the first meaningful oversold reaction
//  DEEP OVERSOLD   (default 20)  → "CONFIRM BUY"   — momentum is stretched to a downside extreme
//
//  The outer pair are confirmation levels, reached only when momentum is genuinely exhausted.
//  The inner pair are early-reaction levels, reached far more often. Reading them together lets
//  you separate a routine pullback from a real momentum extreme at a glance.
//
//  Every signal is printed twice, at the same bar: once in the RSI pane where the level was
//  touched, and once on the candlestick chart above or below the candle. You never have to look
//  away from price to know what the oscillator just did.
//
//  The RSI line itself is coloured by its own slope — green while momentum is rising, red while
//  it is falling — so the direction of the oscillator is readable without tracing the line.
//
//  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
//  │  A N T I - R E P E A T   S T A T E   M A C H I N E                                      │
//  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
//  A naive level check fires on every bar the oscillator spends beyond a level, which buries the
//  chart in duplicate labels and makes alerts unusable. Each of the four levels here carries its
//  own armed / disarmed state. A level fires once when it is crossed and then disarms itself.
//
//  How it re-arms is the main frequency control of the whole tool:
//
//    MIDLINE RESET (default, recommended)
//      A level that has fired stays silent until RSI travels all the way back across the midline.
//      An overbought or resistance signal therefore cannot repeat until momentum has genuinely
//      rotated back down through neutral, and a support or oversold signal cannot repeat until
//      momentum has rotated back up. The practical result is roughly one signal per momentum
//      swing instead of one per level touch, which is what keeps fast intraday charts readable.
//
//    BUFFER RESET
//      With midline reset off, a level re-arms as soon as RSI pulls back past it by the reset
//      buffer. This produces many more signals and suits traders who want every level reaction.
//
//  A per-level minimum bar gap runs on top of both modes as a final guard against clustering.
//
//  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
//  │  U N I V E R S A L   C O M P A T I B I L I T Y                                          │
//  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
//  RSI is a bounded 0-100 oscillator by construction, so no volatility or price scaling is
//  required. Nothing in this script references a raw price value, a pip size or a fixed price
//  distance — all logic lives entirely inside RSI's own 0-100 domain. The same four levels are
//  therefore valid on Forex, gold, indices, crypto and equities, from one-minute charts to
//  monthly, with no adjustment. Price-chart labels are anchored using automatic above-bar and
//  below-bar placement, so they position correctly on any instrument regardless of its price
//  magnitude.
//
//  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
//  │  R E A L - T I M E   B E H A V I O U R   ( I M P O R T A N T )                          │
//  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
//  This is a live-signal tool by design. Level touches are evaluated on the currently forming
//  bar rather than being gated behind bar close, so a signal appears the moment RSI reaches a
//  level instead of one full bar later. The trade-off is standard and expected for any live
//  oscillator tool: on the last, still-forming bar a signal can appear and then withdraw if RSI
//  falls back below the level before that bar closes. Once a bar closes, its signals are final
//  and never change. This is intended behaviour, not a fault. Traders who prefer only settled
//  signals can simply act on closed candles.
//
//  No request.security() call is used anywhere, so there is no higher-timeframe lookahead and no
//  associated repainting risk of any kind.
//
//  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
//  │  D I S C L A I M E R                                                                    │
//  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
//  This tool is for educational and informational purposes only. It does not constitute
//  financial advice. Trading involves risk; past patterns do not guarantee future results. Use
//  proper risk management and consult a licensed financial advisor before trading real capital.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

indicator("RSI Trade Entries", "RSI TE", overlay = false, max_labels_count = 500, max_lines_count = 100)

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  I N P U T S
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gRsi = "═════  RSI SETTINGS  ═════"
rsiLen  = input.int(14,      "RSI Length", minval = 1, maxval = 500, group = gRsi)
rsiSrc  = input.source(close, "RSI Source",                          group = gRsi)

gLvl = "═════  SIGNAL LEVELS  ═════"
obLvl   = input.float(80, "Overbought Level  →  CONFIRM SELL", minval = 50, maxval = 100, step = 0.5, group = gLvl)
resLvl  = input.float(68, "Resistance Level  →  SELL",         minval = 50, maxval = 100, step = 0.5, group = gLvl)
supLvl  = input.float(35, "Support Level     →  BUY",          minval = 0,  maxval = 50,  step = 0.5, group = gLvl)
osLvl   = input.float(20, "Oversold Level    →  CONFIRM BUY",  minval = 0,  maxval = 50,  step = 0.5, group = gLvl)
midReset = input.bool(true, "Reset Levels At Midline (one signal per swing)", group = gLvl, tooltip = "ON  : after a level fires it stays silent until RSI travels back across the midline. This is the main signal-frequency control and gives roughly one signal per momentum swing.\nOFF : a level re-arms as soon as RSI pulls back past it by the buffer below, which produces many more signals.")
midLvl  = input.float(50, "Midline Level", minval = 20, maxval = 80, step = 1, group = gLvl)
resetBf = input.float(3.0, "Level Reset Buffer (RSI points)", minval = 0, maxval = 20, step = 0.5, group = gLvl, tooltip = "Used when midline reset is OFF. How far RSI must pull back past a level before it may fire again.")
minGap  = input.int(3, "Minimum Bars Between Repeats", minval = 0, maxval = 200, group = gLvl, tooltip = "Applied per level. Blocks the same level from firing again within this many bars.")

gSig = "═════  SIGNAL TOGGLES  ═════"
useOb   = input.bool(true, "Enable CONFIRM SELL (Overbought)", group = gSig)
useRes  = input.bool(true, "Enable SELL (Resistance)",         group = gSig)
useSup  = input.bool(true, "Enable BUY (Support)",             group = gSig)
useOs   = input.bool(true, "Enable CONFIRM BUY (Oversold)",    group = gSig)

gLbl = "═════  LABELS  ═════"
lblChart = input.bool(true, "Show Labels On Price Chart", group = gLbl)
lblPane  = input.bool(true, "Show Labels On RSI Pane",    group = gLbl)
maxLbls  = input.int(50,    "Max Labels To Keep",         minval = 4, maxval = 240, group = gLbl, tooltip = "Oldest labels are removed once this count is exceeded, keeping the chart light.")
lblSize  = input.string("Small", "Label Size", options = ["Tiny", "Small", "Normal", "Large"], group = gLbl)
txtOb    = input.string("CONFIRM SELL", "Overbought Text", group = gLbl, inline = "t1")
txtRes   = input.string("SELL",         "Resistance Text", group = gLbl, inline = "t1")
txtSup   = input.string("BUY",          "Support Text",    group = gLbl, inline = "t2")
txtOs    = input.string("CONFIRM BUY",  "Oversold Text",   group = gLbl, inline = "t2")

gCol = "═════  RSI LINE COLOURING  ═════"
smoothOn  = input.bool(false, "Colour Smoothing (reduce flicker)", group = gCol, tooltip = "Colours the line from a smoothed slope instead of a single-bar change, so tiny one-tick wiggles do not flip the colour.")
smoothLen = input.int(3, "Smoothing Length", minval = 1, maxval = 50, group = gCol)
lineW     = input.int(2, "RSI Line Width",   minval = 1, maxval = 5,  group = gCol)

gSty = "═════  STYLE  ═════"
colUp    = input.color(#26a69a, "Bullish", group = gSty, inline = "c1")
colDn    = input.color(#ef5350, "Bearish", group = gSty, inline = "c1")
colBuy   = input.color(#26a69a, "Buy Label",  group = gSty, inline = "c2")
colSell  = input.color(#ef5350, "Sell Label", group = gSty, inline = "c2")
colLblTx = input.color(color.white, "Label Text", group = gSty)
showLvls = input.bool(true, "Show Level Lines",  group = gSty, inline = "c3")
showLvlN = input.bool(true, "Show Level Names",  group = gSty, inline = "c3")
colLvl   = input.color(#b2b5be, "Level Line Colour", group = gSty, inline = "c4")
lvlWidth = input.int(1, "Width", minval = 1, maxval = 4, group = gSty, inline = "c4")

string szLbl = lblSize == "Tiny" ? size.tiny : lblSize == "Normal" ? size.normal : lblSize == "Large" ? size.large : size.small

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  C O R E   —   R S I   A N D   D Y N A M I C   C O L O U R
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
float rsi = ta.rsi(rsiSrc, rsiLen)

// slope of the oscillator itself decides the colour of every plotted segment
float rawSlope = rsi - rsi[1]
float slope    = smoothOn ? ta.ema(rawSlope, smoothLen) : rawSlope
color rsiCol   = slope >= 0 ? colUp : colDn

plot(rsi, "RSI", color = rsiCol, linewidth = lineW)

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  L E V E L   L I N E S
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
color lvlPaint = showLvls ? colLvl : color.new(color.gray, 100)

hline(obLvl,  "Overbought", color = lvlPaint, linestyle = hline.style_solid, linewidth = lvlWidth)
hline(resLvl, "Resistance", color = lvlPaint, linestyle = hline.style_solid, linewidth = lvlWidth)
hline(supLvl, "Support",    color = lvlPaint, linestyle = hline.style_solid, linewidth = lvlWidth)
hline(osLvl,  "Oversold",   color = lvlPaint, linestyle = hline.style_solid, linewidth = lvlWidth)
hline(midLvl, "Midline",    color = showLvls ? color.new(colLvl, 70) : color.new(color.gray, 100), linestyle = hline.style_dotted, linewidth = 1)

// on-pane level names, refreshed on the last bar only
var label nmOb  = na
var label nmRes = na
var label nmSup = na
var label nmOs  = na

if barstate.islast and showLvlN and showLvls
    label.delete(nmOb)
    label.delete(nmRes)
    label.delete(nmSup)
    label.delete(nmOs)
    nmOb  := label.new(bar_index + 3, obLvl,  "Overbought", xloc = xloc.bar_index, style = label.style_none, textcolor = colLvl, size = size.tiny)
    nmRes := label.new(bar_index + 3, resLvl, "Resistance", xloc = xloc.bar_index, style = label.style_none, textcolor = colLvl, size = size.tiny)
    nmSup := label.new(bar_index + 3, supLvl, "Support",    xloc = xloc.bar_index, style = label.style_none, textcolor = colLvl, size = size.tiny)
    nmOs  := label.new(bar_index + 3, osLvl,  "Oversold",   xloc = xloc.bar_index, style = label.style_none, textcolor = colLvl, size = size.tiny)

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  A N T I - R E P E A T   S T A T E   M A C H I N E
//  Each level fires once on the cross, then stays disarmed until RSI pulls back past it by the
//  reset buffer. This is what keeps one touch equal to one label.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
var bool armOb  = true
var bool armRes = true
var bool armSup = true
var bool armOs  = true

var int lastOb  = -100000
var int lastRes = -100000
var int lastSup = -100000
var int lastOs  = -100000

bool sigOb  = false
bool sigRes = false
bool sigSup = false
bool sigOs  = false

// ── UPPER LEVELS — crossed from below ────────────────────────────────────────
if useOb and armOb and rsi >= obLvl and rsi[1] < obLvl and (bar_index - lastOb) >= minGap
    sigOb  := true
    armOb  := false
    lastOb := bar_index
if not armOb and (midReset ? rsi < midLvl : rsi < obLvl - resetBf)
    armOb := true

if useRes and armRes and rsi >= resLvl and rsi[1] < resLvl and (bar_index - lastRes) >= minGap
    sigRes  := true
    armRes  := false
    lastRes := bar_index
if not armRes and (midReset ? rsi < midLvl : rsi < resLvl - resetBf)
    armRes := true

// ── LOWER LEVELS — crossed from above ────────────────────────────────────────
if useSup and armSup and rsi <= supLvl and rsi[1] > supLvl and (bar_index - lastSup) >= minGap
    sigSup  := true
    armSup  := false
    lastSup := bar_index
if not armSup and (midReset ? rsi > midLvl : rsi > supLvl + resetBf)
    armSup := true

if useOs and armOs and rsi <= osLvl and rsi[1] > osLvl and (bar_index - lastOs) >= minGap
    sigOs  := true
    armOs  := false
    lastOs := bar_index
if not armOs and (midReset ? rsi > midLvl : rsi > osLvl + resetBf)
    armOs := true

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  D U A L - P A N E   L A B E L   S Y S T E M
//  Every signal prints twice on the same bar: once in the RSI pane at the level it touched, and
//  once on the candlestick chart via force_overlay, above the candle for sells and below it for
//  buys. Above-bar and below-bar anchoring is used on the price chart so the labels position
//  correctly on any instrument without referencing raw price values.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
var array<label> store = array.new<label>()

emit(bool fire, string txt, color bg, bool isSell, float paneY) =>
    if fire
        if lblPane
            store.push(label.new(bar_index, paneY, txt, xloc = xloc.bar_index, style = isSell ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up, color = bg, textcolor = colLblTx, size = szLbl))
        if lblChart
            store.push(label.new(bar_index, na, txt, xloc = xloc.bar_index, yloc = isSell ? yloc.abovebar : yloc.belowbar, style = isSell ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up, color = bg, textcolor = colLblTx, size = szLbl, force_overlay = true))

emit(sigOb,  txtOb,  colSell, true,  rsi)
emit(sigRes, txtRes, colSell, true,  rsi)
emit(sigSup, txtSup, colBuy,  false, rsi)
emit(sigOs,  txtOs,  colBuy,  false, rsi)

// retire the oldest drawings, on confirmed bars only so nothing is lost to real-time rollback
if barstate.isconfirmed
    while store.size() > maxLbls
        label.delete(store.shift())

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  A L E R T S
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
alertcondition(sigOb,  "RSI Overbought — Confirm Sell", "RSI Trade Entries: RSI reached the overbought level on {{ticker}} {{interval}} — confirm sell.")
alertcondition(sigRes, "RSI Resistance — Sell",         "RSI Trade Entries: RSI reached the resistance level on {{ticker}} {{interval}} — sell signal.")
alertcondition(sigOs,  "RSI Oversold — Confirm Buy",    "RSI Trade Entries: RSI reached the oversold level on {{ticker}} {{interval}} — confirm buy.")
alertcondition(sigSup, "RSI Support — Buy",             "RSI Trade Entries: RSI reached the support level on {{ticker}} {{interval}} — buy signal.")

if sigOb
    alert("RSI Confirm Sell — " + syminfo.ticker + " " + timeframe.period, alert.freq_once_per_bar)
if sigRes
    alert("RSI Sell — " + syminfo.ticker + " " + timeframe.period, alert.freq_once_per_bar)
if sigSup
    alert("RSI Buy — " + syminfo.ticker + " " + timeframe.period, alert.freq_once_per_bar)
if sigOs
    alert("RSI Confirm Buy — " + syminfo.ticker + " " + timeframe.period, alert.freq_once_per_bar)

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  D A T A   W I N D O W   O U T P U T S
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
plot(slope, "RSI Slope", color = color.new(color.orange, 100), display = display.data_window)
plot((sigOb or sigRes) ? -1 : (sigSup or sigOs) ? 1 : 0, "Signal", color = color.new(color.orange, 100), display = display.data_window)